        "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion",
        "problem": "Expand ${( 3x + y )}^3$ using the binomial theorem.",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3,$ we have  $a=3x,$ $b=y,$ and $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+y)^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3-1}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3-2}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3-3}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1)+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3x)^{3}+3\\cdot (3x)^{2}(y)+3\\cdot (3x)(y)^{2}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot ({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}y+9x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
